      "content": [
        {
          "type": "output_text",
          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"lawrence-women-in-love-37dd4c8aeee9268b\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.9,\"analysis\":\"The repeated words are principally a sincere avowal of Birkin’s love: the narrator explicitly says he speaks “with truth.” Their marital setting gives them a faint undert undertaking resonance, but the phrase does not independently institute or promise the relationship. Repetition, whispering, trembling, and intense gladness do not establish expressive/reflexive force. The narrator’s claim that the formula fails to capture a transcendent oneness qualifies the avowal’s adequacy without changing its core force into an ontological remainder.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The surrounding marriage and language of union weakly support P. The narrator’s metaphysical rejection of “I” and “you” creates some pressure toward O, but it concerns the conventional proposition’s inability to describe the full experience, not a distinct core speech-act force.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Birkin directly whispers the repeated avowal “I love you, I love you” to Ursula; it is presented within third-person narration.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Birkin sincerely loves Ursula, but the narrator presents ordinary love-language as radically inadequate to what the moment means to him: rebirth, gratitude, self-transcendence, and a union in which separate “I” and “you” disappear. Ursula cannot perceive this significance and instead wants more explicit proof and adoration. Thus the utterance is truthfully meant yet fails, in the narrative’s metaphysical framing, to communicate Birkin’s whole experience.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Even when he said, whispering with truth, ‘I love you, I love you,’”\",\"supports\":\"The explicit attribution of truth strongly supports a sincere truth-conditional avowal; whispering and repetition alone do not establish E.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“it was not the real truth. It was something beyond love, such a gladness of having surpassed oneself, of having transcended the old existence.”\",\"supports\":\"The words inadequately represent Birkin’s larger transformative experience, while the contrast with “real truth” qualifies adequacy rather than negating sincere T force.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“This I, this old formula of the age, was a dead letter,” followed by the description of a “new oneness” beyond separate “I” and “you.”\",\"supports\":\"Explains the metaphysical and linguistic pressure on the formula, but does not by itself establish a separate speech-act force requiring O.\",\"confidence\":0.95},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Birkin says, “We’re married as much today as we shall be tomorrow”; the legal marriage follows the next day.\",\"supports\":\"The relationship and marriage context provide weak support for hearing the avowal as relationally confirmatory, though not as an independent undertaking.\",\"confidence\":0.93},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Ursula “wanted proof, and statement, even over-statement,” and could not know how much the words meant to him.\",\"supports\":\"Shows the communicative mismatch: she receives the phrase as an insufficient statement, while its private significance for him is much greater.\",\"confidence\":0.96}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T/P/E adequately classifies the utterance’s core force as a sincere avowal with slight relational-undertaking resonance. The passage’s insistence that the formula cannot express self-transcending oneness is important contextual and semantic pressure, but not a distinct recurrent force that requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"E is kept at zero because the text supplies intensity and repetition but no positive indication that the words emerge as a reflex-like discharge rather than as a deliberately meaningful avowal.\"}"
        }
      ],
